{"authored" [{"question" "q-19493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of ${x}^{1/3}$ about $x=8$ to approximate the value of ${7}^{1/3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-19493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=8.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/3} \\\\ f(8) & ={8}^{1/3}=2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{3}(8)^{-2/3}=\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{1/3} & =2+\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4}(x-8)+⋯ \\\\  & =2+\\frac{x-8}{12}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=7$ into the above to approximate ${7}^{1/3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{1/3} & ≈2+\\frac{7-8}{12} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-19493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bde5d83c6d9bf036343499a4eb80396ce923eed9