a2bb225236fd5b669ebd9bc7870aa927927692d39"} {"question" "q-19493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=8.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/3} \\\\ f(8) & ={8}^{1/3}=2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{3}(8)^{-2/3}=\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{1/3} & =2+\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4}(x-8)+⋯ \\\\  & =2+\\frac{x-8}{12}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=7$ into the above to approximate ${7}^{1/3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{1/3} & ≈2+\\frac{7-8}{12} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/state.json" "file_sha256" "023dc12e7568ada1988fc04a2bb225236fd5b669ebd9bc7870aa927927692d39"} {"question" "q-19493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c793865b5210c90347b4fc71cce222ea8d65d390ee7089d5905c0a026001588b"} {"question" "q-19493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/state.json" "file_sha256" "023dc12e7568ada1988fc04a2bb225236fd5b669ebd9bc7870aa927927692d39"} {"question" "q-19493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{23}{12}"] ["math" "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{25}{12}"] ["math" "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{4}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/state.json" "file_sha256" "023dc12e7568ada1988fc04a2bb225236fd5b669ebd9bc7870aa927927692d39"} {"question" "q-19493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{23}{12}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/state.json" "file_sha256" "023dc12e7568ada1988fc04a2bb225236fd5b669ebd9bc7870aa927927692d39"} {"question" "q-83193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ about the point $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/state.json" "file_sha256" "023dc12e7568ada1988fc04a2bb225236fd5b669ebd9bc7870aa927927692d39"} {"question" "q-83193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ and $a=1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\ f(1) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x}={x}^{-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =(1)^{-1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=-{x}^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-(1)^{-2}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}=2{x}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(1) & =2(1)^{-3}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =0+(1)(x-1)+\\frac{(-1)}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{2}{3!}(x-1)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =(x-1)-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/state.json" "file_sha256" "023dc12e7568ada1988fc04a2bb225236fd5b669ebd9bc7870aa927927692d39"} {"question" "q-83193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c793865b5210c90347b4fc71cce222ea8d65d390ee7089d5905c0a026001588b"} {"question" "q-83193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/state.json" "file_sha256" "023dc12e7568ada1988fc04a2bb225236fd5b669ebd9bc7870aa927927692d39"} {"question" "q-83193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=(x-1)+(x-1)^{2}+2(x-1)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=(x-1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{6}+⋯"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=(x-1)-(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{2}+⋯"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=(x-1)-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{3}+⋯"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=(x-1)-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{6}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/state.json" "file_sha256" "023dc12e7568ada1988fc04a2bb225236fd5b669ebd9bc7870aa927927692d39"} {"question" "q-83193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=(x-1)-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{3}+⋯"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/state.json" "file_sha256" "023dc12e7568ada1988fc04a2bb225236fd5b669ebd9bc7870aa927927692d39"} {"question" "q-126848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x$ about $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/state.json" "file_sha256" "023dc12e7568ada1988fc04a2bb225236fd5b669ebd9bc7870aa927927692d39"} {"question" "q-126848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x$ and $a=1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x \\\\ f(1) & =(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x-\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi x \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -(1)\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =-1+0=-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi x-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi x-{\\pi }^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x=-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi x-{\\pi }^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -{\\pi }^{2}(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =0+{\\pi }^{2}={\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x & =-1-(x-1)+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =-1-(x-1)+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/state.json" "file_sha256" "023dc12e7568ada1988fc04a2bb225236fd5b669ebd9bc7870aa927927692d39"} {"question" "q-126848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c793865b5210c90347b4fc71cce222ea8d65d390ee7089d5905c0a026001588b"} {"question" "q-126848" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/state.json" "file_sha256" "023dc12e7568ada1988fc04a2bb225236fd5b669ebd9bc7870aa927927692d39"} {"question" "q-126848" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x=(x-1)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}(x-1)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x=(x-1)-(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}(x-1)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x=-1+2(x-1)-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}(x-1)^{2}+⋯"] ["math" "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x=-1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}(x-1)^{2}+⋯"] ["math" "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x=-1-(x-1)+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}(x-1)^{2}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/state.json" "file_sha256" "023dc12e7568ada1988fc04a2bb225236fd5b669ebd9bc7870aa927927692d39"} {"question" "q-126848" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x=-1-(x-1)+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}(x-1)^{2}+⋯"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/state.json" "file_sha256" "023dc12e7568ada1988fc04a2bb225236fd5b669ebd9bc7870aa927927692d39"} {"question" "q-353900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $(x+1){e}^{x}$ about $x=-2.$\n\n$(x+1){e}^{x}=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x+2)^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x+2)^{3}+⋯$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/state.json" "file_sha256" "023dc12e7568ada1988fc04a2bb225236fd5b669ebd9bc7870aa927927692d39"} {"question" "q-353900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=(x+1){e}^{x}$ and $a=-2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+1){e}^{x} \\\\ f(-2) & =(-2+1){e}^{-2}=-{e}^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}+(x+1){e}^{x}=(2+x){e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-2) & =(2-2){e}^{-2}=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}+(2+x){e}^{x}=(3+x){e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =(3-2){e}^{-2}={e}^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & ={e}^{x}+(3+x){e}^{x}=(4+x){e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(-2) & =(4-2){e}^{-2}=2{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1){e}^{x} & =-{e}^{-2}+(0)(x+2)+\\frac{{e}^{-2}}{2!}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{2{e}^{-2}}{3!}(x+2)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3{e}^{2}}(x+2)^{3}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/state.json" "file_sha256" "023dc12e7568ada1988fc04a2bb225236fd5b669ebd9bc7870aa927927692d39"} {"question" "q-353900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c793865b5210c90347b4fc71cce222ea8d65d390ee7089d5905c0a026001588b"} {"question" "q-353900" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062975/state.json" "file_sha256" "023dc12e7568ada1988fc04a2bb225236fd5b669ebd9bc7870aa927927692d39"} {"question" "q-353900" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field